# 5. Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA)

## What is VPA?

Vertical Pod Autoscaler automatically adjusts CPU and memory requests for pods based on resource usage.

Unlike HPA, which increases or decreases pod replicas, VPA changes the resources allocated to individual pods.

**Note:** VPA is not included in Kubernetes by default. It requires separate installation and CRDs.

## What is CRD?

CRD stands for Custom Resource Definition.

It allows us to extend Kubernetes by creating our own resource types beyond built-in resources like Pods, Services, and Deployments.

## VPA Components

### 1. Recommender

- Monitors CPU and memory usage.
- Calculates suitable resource requests.

### 2. Updater

- Identifies pods that need resource changes.
- Evicts pods when required.
- Allows new pods to start with updated resources.

### 3. Admission Controller

- Applies recommended resource values when new pods are created.

## VPA Update Modes

| Mode | Description |
|---|---|
| Off | Provides recommendations only |
| Initial | Applies recommendations when pods are created |
| Recreate | Recreates pods to apply updated resources |
| InPlaceOrRecreate | Attempts in-place updates before recreating pods |

## Install VPA

Install Git:

```bash
yum install -y git
```

Clone the autoscaler repository:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/kubernetes/autoscaler.git
```

Navigate to VPA directory:

```bash
cd autoscaler/vertical-pod-autoscaler
```

Install VPA:

```bash
./hack/vpa-up.sh
```

Check VPA components:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep vpa
```

## Install Metrics Server

```bash
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/high-availability-1.21+.yaml
```

Check:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system | grep metrics-server
```

