# 6. Practical VPA Lab

### Step 1: Create Deployment

```bash
vi deploy.yml
```

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: demo-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: demo
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: demo
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: demo-container
        image: polinux/stress
        command: ["stress"]
        args: ["--cpu", "1", "--vm", "1", "--vm-bytes", "128M"]
        resources:
          requests:
            cpu: "50m"
            memory: "50Mi"
```

Apply deployment:

```bash
kubectl apply -f deploy.yml
```

Check metrics:

```bash
kubectl top pods
```

Check resource requests:

```bash
kubectl describe pods -l app=demo | grep -A3 "Requests:"
```

### Step 2: Create VPA Object

```bash
vi vpa.yml
```

```yaml
apiVersion: autoscaling.k8s.io/v1
kind: VerticalPodAutoscaler
metadata:
  name: demo-vpa
spec:
  targetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: demo-app
  updatePolicy:
    updateMode: "Recreate"
  resourcePolicy:
    containerPolicies:
    - containerName: demo-container
      minAllowed:
        cpu: 50m
        memory: 50Mi
      maxAllowed:
        cpu: "2"
        memory: 500Mi
```

Apply VPA:

```bash
kubectl apply -f vpa.yml
```

### Step 3: Verify VPA

```bash
kubectl get vpa
```

Check VPA checkpoints:

```bash
kubectl get verticalpodautoscalercheckpoints
```

Check recommendations:

```bash
kubectl describe vpa demo-vpa
```

Check pod resource requests:

```bash
kubectl get pods --watch
kubectl describe pod <new-pod-name> | grep -A2 "Requests"
```

### Important VPA Terms

- Uncapped Target: Actual resource usage-based recommendation before applying resource policy limits.
- Lower Bound: Minimum recommended resource value.
- Target: Recommended resource value.
- Upper Bound: Maximum recommended resource value.

### Delete VPA Resources

```bash
kubectl delete -f vpa.yml
kubectl delete -f deploy.yml
```

