## Step 7: Create TriggerAuthentication

```bash
vi triggerauth.yml
```

```yaml
apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1
kind: TriggerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: aws-auth
  namespace: keda-demo
spec:
  secretTargetRef:
  - parameter: awsAccessKeyID
    name: aws-secret
    key: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID
  - parameter: awsSecretAccessKey
    name: aws-secret
    key: AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
```

Apply:

```bash
kubectl apply -f triggerauth.yml
```

TriggerAuthentication connects KEDA with the AWS credentials stored in the Kubernetes Secret.

## Step 8: Create ScaledObject

```bash
vi scaledobject.yml
```

```yaml
apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1
kind: ScaledObject
metadata:
  name: sqs-scaler
  namespace: keda-demo
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    name: sqs-consumer
  pollingInterval: 15
  minReplicaCount: 1
  maxReplicaCount: 10
  triggers:
  - type: aws-sqs-queue
    authenticationRef:
      name: aws-auth
    metadata:
      queueURL: https://sqs.ap-south-1.amazonaws.com/ACCOUNT_ID/orders-queue
      awsRegion: ap-south-1
      queueLength: "5"
```

Apply:

```bash
kubectl apply -f scaledobject.yml
```

### Explanation

- `pollingInterval: 15` – KEDA checks the queue every 15 seconds.
- `minReplicaCount: 1` – Minimum number of pods.
- `maxReplicaCount: 10` – Maximum number of pods.
- `queueLength: "5"` – Target queue length per replica.

