# 11. KEDA Cron Scaling

## What is KEDA Cron Scaling?

KEDA Cron Scaler automatically scales pods based on a predefined schedule.

Unlike CPU-based scaling, it uses time-based rules.

### Real-World Example

Imagine an application used during office hours.

```text
9 AM – 6 PM
      |
      v
  Scale to 5 Pods
      |
      v
After 6 PM
      |
      v
  Scale to 1 Pod
```

This helps reduce resource usage during low-traffic hours.

## Practical Lab

### Step 1: Create Namespace

```bash
kubectl create namespace cron-demo
```

### Step 2: Create Deployment

```bash
vi nginx.yaml
```

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: nginx-demo
  namespace: cron-demo
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: nginx-demo
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: nginx-demo
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx
```

Apply:

```bash
kubectl apply -f nginx.yaml
```

### Step 3: Create Cron ScaledObject

```bash
vi cron-scaler.yaml
```

```yaml
apiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1
kind: ScaledObject
metadata:
  name: cron-scaler
  namespace: cron-demo
spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    name: nginx-demo
  minReplicaCount: 1
  maxReplicaCount: 10
  triggers:
  - type: cron
    metadata:
      timezone: UTC
      start: "17 10 * * *"
      end: "20 10 * * *"
      desiredReplicas: "5"
```

Apply:

```bash
kubectl apply -f cron-scaler.yaml
```

### Step 4: Verify Scaling

```bash
kubectl get scaledobject -n cron-demo
```

```bash
kubectl get hpa -n cron-demo
```

```bash
kubectl get pods -n cron-demo -w
```

**Note:** The example uses UTC timezone. Update the schedule and timezone according to your requirements.

